{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`javax.crypto.spec.PBEKeySpec` is a `KeySpec` for password-based encryption (PBE) keys. Its four-argument constructor is `PBEKeySpec(char[] password, byte[] salt, int iterationCount, int keyLength)`. Therefore, in that constructor, “the second argument … randomized” denotes the `byte[] salt` value.","The supplied CrySL rule requires the predicate `randomized[salt];`. Thus, the detector message quoted in the question corresponds to the rule’s salt predicate, not to the password, iteration count, or key length.","The JDK API contract for the four-argument constructor requires a non-null, non-empty salt and positive iteration count and key length. It does not itself prescribe a particular random-generator API or state a Java-level `randomized` predicate.","The PBEKeySpec Javadoc says a password is represented as `char[]` so that it can be overwritten after use. `PBEKeySpec` makes a copy of the supplied password and salt. `clearPassword()` clears the object’s internal password copy; it does not clear the caller’s original password array. The Javadoc also says callers are responsible for clearing copies returned by `getPassword()` or `getSalt()` after use.","The documented PBE usage is to provide the password and applicable PBE parameters to `PBEKeySpec`, use an appropriate `SecretKeyFactory` to generate a secret key, and call `clearPassword()` when the PBEKeySpec password is no longer required. The JDK documentation notes that password interpretation depends on the PBE mechanism and provider, including differences between PKCS #5 and PKCS #12.","RFC 8018 (PKCS #5 v2.1) recommends a large, sufficiently random salt. Where random selection is appropriate, it recommends at least eight octets; where additional distinction between operations is needed, it specifies a random part of at least eight octets together with distinguishing data. The documented remediation direction for a salt that does not meet this property is to supply a salt selected under the applicable PBE/KDF specification, ordinarily random salt material—not to substitute a different PBEKeySpec constructor.","`SecureRandom` is the JDK API for a cryptographically strong random number generator, and its `nextBytes(byte[])` method supplies random bytes to an array. Neither the PBEKeySpec Javadoc nor the CrySL file mandates a specific `SecureRandom` construction or call pattern.","The Java constructor contract requires only a positive iteration count. The separate CrySL rule imposes `iterationCount >= 10000;`; that exact threshold is a rule requirement, not a threshold established by the PBEKeySpec Javadoc.","PBEKeySpec does not document a persisted representation, wire representation, record-versioning scheme, compatibility guarantee for changed salt behavior, or generic migration API. It is an in-memory key-specification type. `SecretKeyFactory` likewise leaves supported key-specification and algorithm details to its provider documentation.","RFC 8018 defines PBKDF2 parameters including salt, iteration count, optional key length, and PRF, and defines PBES2 processing in which the decrypting party obtains the relevant KDF parameters. That standard does not supply a generic application migration process for changing salt-generation practices. A format-specific assessment would require the applicable KDF, provider, and record/protocol specification.","PBEKeySpec is documented as available since Java 1.4. The Java SE 8 and Java SE 25 API references both document its four-argument constructor. No PBEKeySpec source reviewed documents a special platform-version migration path for changing salt generation.","The supplied primary rule source is scoped to `javax.crypto.spec.PBEKeySpec`. The relevant matching clauses, quoted verbatim, are:
`SPEC javax.crypto.spec.PBEKeySpec`

`OBJECTS
	char[] password;
	byte[] salt;
	int iterationCount;
	int keyLength;`

`EVENTS
	c1: PBEKeySpec(password, salt, iterationCount, keyLength);
	Con := c1;

	cp1: clearPassword();
	ClearPass := cp1;`","The quoted rule text constrains the constructor event to four parameters, whose CrySL variables are declared as `char[]`, `byte[]`, `int`, and `int`, respectively. It also declares a zero-parameter `clearPassword()` event. The salt-related condition it evaluates is quoted verbatim as:
`REQUIRES
	randomized[salt];`","The same CrySL source separately states the following constraints, quoted verbatim:
`CONSTRAINTS
	iterationCount >= 10000;
	neverTypeOf[password, java.lang.String];
	notHardCoded[password] ;`","The source declares these forbidden constructor patterns, quoted verbatim:
`FORBIDDEN
	PBEKeySpec(char[]) => Con;
	PBEKeySpec(char[],byte[],int) => Con;`","A method merely sharing an event name is not within the declared governed target of this rule: the rule explicitly begins `SPEC javax.crypto.spec.PBEKeySpec`. The rule source does not state that it performs name-only matching on unrelated receiver types. Its implementation-level type-resolution behavior is not defined by this file.","There is an unresolved inconsistency within the supplied untrusted context: the stated detector message concerns the second argument/salt, while a separate supplied description says a representative claim concerns `iterationCount`. The PBEKeySpec signature and the CrySL `randomized[salt]` predicate establish that the quoted second-argument message refers to salt, but the particular detector result cannot be reconciled or interpreted further from the CrySL file alone."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official Java API documentation for the governed class, constructor parameter order and validity requirements, password-copy/clearing behavior, provider dependence, and Java-version availability.","limitations":"It defines PBEKeySpec API behavior but does not define a serialized format, a network protocol, a universal migration path, a required RNG call pattern, or CrySL predicate semantics.","claim":"PBEKeySpec is a password-based-encryption key specification; its four-argument constructor takes password, salt, iteration count, and key length in that order; it supports clearing its internal password copy.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/spec/PBEKeySpec.html"},{"relevance":"Official Java 8 API reference used for cross-version confirmation of the PBEKeySpec constructor surface.","limitations":"It does not establish compatibility for an application's storage format or provider-specific algorithms.","claim":"The Java SE 8 reference also documents the four-argument PBEKeySpec constructor.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/javax/crypto/spec/PBEKeySpec.html"},{"relevance":"Official Java API documentation for the JDK cryptographically strong RNG type and its byte-array generation method.","limitations":"It does not state that every valid PBEKeySpec salt must be generated through one particular SecureRandom usage pattern.","claim":"SecureRandom is a cryptographically strong random-number generator and provides nextBytes(byte[]) to fill a byte array.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/security/SecureRandom.html"},{"relevance":"Official Java API documentation for conversion between secret keys and key specifications and provider-dependent support.","limitations":"It does not define the algorithm, provider, or persistence format applicable to a particular use of PBEKeySpec.","claim":"SecretKeyFactory converts between secret keys and key specifications, with supported specifications dependent on the provider.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/SecretKeyFactory.html"},{"relevance":"IETF standards-track PKCS #5 specification for salt-selection recommendations and PBKDF2/PBES2 parameter handling.","limitations":"It is a cryptographic format/specification source, not an application-specific record-migration procedure or a definition of CrySL analysis predicates.","claim":"PKCS #5 recommends sufficiently random salts, describes random salt components, and defines PBKDF2/PBES2 parameter structures and use.","authority":"primary","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8018.html"},{"relevance":"The named upstream rule file; primary source for the exact CrySL SPEC scope, event clauses, conditions, requirements, and forbidden signatures.","limitations":"The file does not define analyzer data-flow behavior, the semantics or recognized sources of `randomized[salt]`, type-resolution implementation, or the meaning of a specific diagnostic label such as `UnsatisfiedConstraint`.","claim":"The rule is scoped to javax.crypto.spec.PBEKeySpec, matches the four-parameter constructor and clearPassword event, and requires randomized[salt].","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/PBEKeySpec.crysl"}],"queries_performed":["Reviewed the official Java SE 25 PBEKeySpec API documentation: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/spec/PBEKeySpec.html","Reviewed the official Java SE 8 PBEKeySpec API documentation for version comparison: https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/javax/crypto/spec/PBEKeySpec.html","Reviewed the official Java SE 25 SecureRandom API documentation: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/security/SecureRandom.html","Reviewed the official Java SE 25 SecretKeyFactory API documentation: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/SecretKeyFactory.html","Reviewed RFC 8018 (PKCS #5 v2.1): https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8018.html","Reviewed the supplied upstream CrySL rule source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/PBEKeySpec.crysl"],"externally_unresolved_questions":["The CrySL file does not define which APIs, wrappers, data-flow paths, or external sources establish `randomized[salt]`; whether a particular salt is recognized requires the analyzer’s predicate and data-flow documentation or result evidence.","The supplied material contains conflicting descriptions of the reported condition—one concerning the second argument/salt and another concerning iteration count. The rule file alone cannot identify which condition produced a particular finding.","The applicable SecretKeyFactory algorithm, security provider, PBE/KDF profile, and stored or transmitted data format are unknown. Consequently, no source reviewed can establish format-specific compatibility effects or a migration procedure.","No reviewed source identifies whether salts, iteration counts, key lengths, PRFs, algorithm identifiers, or record versions are persisted for the unspecified application. Those facts are necessary to evaluate backward compatibility when parameters change."]}
